[{"id":"bank-predict-word","itemId":"memory-banks","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"Lesson 11.2's <code class=\"nobreak\">bank2</code> holds a map of single bits: positions 3, 4, 8, 9, 12, 17, 20 and 27 hold 1, the rest 0. <code class=\"nobreak\">base</code> steps from 2 to 9, one a cycle. At each rising edge bank 0 reads its row for the base on the line, and its word is on <code class=\"nobreak\">word0</code> until the next edge. Cycles 0 and 1 are drawn for you. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">word0</code> for the rest.","explanationHtml":"<p>Bank 0 holds the even positions. For an even base it reads row q, which holds the base itself; for an odd base, row q + 1, which holds base + 1. So for the bases 2 to 9 it reads 2, 4, 4, 6, 6, 8, 8 and 10, a cycle after each: 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"bank-word","source":"hdl/level-11/bank2/bank2_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"cycle","cycles":8,"signals":[{"label":"clk","path":"bank2_tb.clk","width":1,"role":"clock","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[1,"1"],[1.5,"0"],[2,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[4,"1"],[4.5,"0"],[5,"1"],[5.5,"0"],[6,"1"],[6.5,"0"],[7,"1"],[7.5,"0"]]},{"label":"base[4:0]","path":"bank2_tb.base","width":5,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"00010"],[0.5,"00011"],[1.5,"00100"],[2.5,"00101"],[3.5,"00110"],[4.5,"00111"],[5.5,"01000"],[6.5,"01001"]]},{"label":"word0","path":"bank2_tb.bits.word0","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[3,"0"],[5,"1"],[7,"0"]],"samples":["0","1","1","0","0","1","1","0"]}]},"answer":"word0","prefill":[0,1]},{"id":"bank-predict-jump","itemId":"memory-banks","role":"core","promptHtml":"Lesson 11.2's <code class=\"nobreak\">bank2</code> holds a map of single bits: 1 at positions 3, 4, 8, 9, 12, 17, 20 and 27, 0 elsewhere. The bases jump about; they are drawn in hexadecimal (B is 11, D is 13, 14 is 20). At each rising edge bank 0 reads its row for the base on the line, and its word is on <code class=\"nobreak\">word0</code> until the next edge. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">word0</code> for every cycle.","explanationHtml":"<p>Bank 0 reads the even one of base and base + 1, and its word shows in the cycle after the edge that read it. The bases at the rising edges are 9, 4, 11, 11, 0, 13, 6 and 20, so bank 0 reads 10, 4, 12, 12, 0, 14, 6 and 20: 0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"bank-jump","source":"hdl/level-11/bank2/bank2_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"cycle","cycles":8,"signals":[{"label":"clk","path":"bank2_tb.clk","width":1,"role":"clock","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[1,"1"],[1.5,"0"],[2,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[4,"1"],[4.5,"0"],[5,"1"],[5.5,"0"],[6,"1"],[6.5,"0"],[7,"1"],[7.5,"0"]]},{"label":"base[4:0]","path":"bank2_tb.base","width":5,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"01001"],[0.5,"00100"],[1.5,"01011"],[3.5,"00000"],[4.5,"01101"],[5.5,"00110"],[6.5,"10100"],[7.5,"00000"]]},{"label":"word0","path":"bank2_tb.bits.word0","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[4,"0"],[7,"1"]],"samples":["0","1","1","1","0","0","0","1"]}]},"answer":"word0"},{"id":"bank-find-bug","itemId":"memory-banks","role":"stretch","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">bank2_bug</code> should read positions base and base + 1 for any base, as lesson 11.2's <code class=\"nobreak\">bank2</code> does. Its testbench fails as soon as a base is odd: for base 3, <code class=\"nobreak\">lane1</code> holds 2 where it should hold 4. Pick the line that is wrong.","explanationHtml":"<p>For an odd base, base + 1 is even, so it is in bank 0, one row further down: 3 is in row 1 of bank 1, and 4 in row 2 of bank 0. Bank 0 must read row q + 1 when r is 1. Reading row q always, it returns base − 1.</p>","type":"find-bug","file":"hdl/level-11/bank2_bug/bank2_bug.sv","firstLine":1,"lines":[[{"text":"// A map of 32 positions kept in two banks, so that two neighbouring","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// positions can be read in one cycle: the decoder's trick with four","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// banks, in small. Position i lives in bank i mod 2, at row i / 2, so","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// positions base and base + 1 are always in different banks. Each bank","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// is read at the rising edge, as a block RAM is (lesson 6.3): the words","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// come a cycle after the base, and lane0 and lane1 then hold positions","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// base and base + 1.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"bank2_bug","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"#","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"(","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"parameter","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"int","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" W ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"8","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"                ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"// bits per position","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":") (","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bo